*** DNS Conceptual / End-User Layer

* DNS is a Hierarchial Namespace
- Domain names are like folders in a tree, but read from right to left
- www.google.com
	- com (Top-Level Domain TLD)
		- google (domain)
			- www (Hostname / Label)
- The root of the DNS system is just "." - but you usually don't see it
	- www.google.com. (the trailing dot is real, and in DNS terminology is called the 'actual full name')

* Zones, Not Domains
- A domain is a name
- A zone is the piece of the namespace that someone controls and manages
- Example:
	- com is a zone (managed by Verisign)
	- google.com is a zone (managed by Google)
	- www.google.com is not a zone. It is a record inside the google.com zone
	- ZONE: google.com
	- Contains: A/AAA/MX/TXT/CNAME/etc.

* Delegation
- How control is passed down the tree
- "Who knows google.com?" 
	- Root -> .com -> google.com
- Each layer points to the next layer using NS (name server) records and sometimes glue records
- At .com there is a record saying:
	- google.com.  NS  ns1.google.com
	- google.com.  NS  ns2.google.com
- This tells resolvers: "Ask these authoritative servers for google.com"

* Authoritative vs Recursive Resolvers
Type			What it does							Example Software
Authoritative Server	Holds the real zone data					BIND, NSD, Knot, PowerDNS-Authoritative
Recursive Resolver	Looks up answers on behalf of clients, following the chain	Unbound, BIND (as resolver), dnsmasq, Cloudflare 1.1.1.1
- Clients don't contact authoritative servers directly. They ask recursive resolvers like (8.8.8.8)

* Common DNS Record Types
- These are resource records inside a zone
Record		Purpose							Example
A		Maps name -> IPv4					example.com -> 93.184.216.34
AAAA		Maps name -> IPv6					exapmle.com -> 2606:2800:220:1:248:1893:25c8:1946
CNAME		Alias/redirect name -> another name			www -> example.com
MX		Mail Server Routing					example.com -> mail.example.com
TXT		Human-readable or verification text			SPF, DKIM, Google site verify
NS		Delegation / authoritative nameserver for zone		Points to servers for the zone
SOA		"Start of Authority" (zone metadata)			Serial, refresh times, etc

* Caching & TTL
- When a resolver gets a DNS answer, it stores it in cache to avoid asking again
- Every record has a TTL (Time to Live) in seconds
- Example: 
	- Example.com.	300	A	93.184.216.34
- TTL = 300 means:
	- Cache for 300 seconds 
	- After that, ask upstream again

* What a Zone File Looks Like
____________________________________________________
$TTL 3600
@       IN SOA  ns1.example.com. admin.example.com. (
                2025020501 ; serial
                3600       ; refresh
                900        ; retry
                1209600    ; expire
                86400 )    ; minimum

        IN NS   ns1.example.com.
        IN NS   ns2.example.com.

@       IN A    203.0.113.10
www     IN A    203.0.113.10
mail    IN A    203.0.113.11

@       IN MX   10 mail.example.com.

_txttest IN TXT "hello world"
___________________________________________________
- @ means "the zone name" (example.com)
- Relative names automatically append the zone (so www means www.example.com)
- Serial should change every time the zone changes (important for replication)

* Putting it All Together (mental Walk-through)
- When the browser looks up www.example.com
	1. Your OS -> Recursive resolver
	2. Resolver checks cache:
		If present and TTL not expired -> returns immidiately
	3. If not cached, resolver:
		Contacts Root -> Gets NS for .com
		Contacts .com -> Gets NS for example.com
		Contacts authoritative NS for example.com
		Gets the A record for www
	4. Resolver returns answer and caches it

* More on Zones
- The Key Idea
	- A domain is just a name in ther hierarchy
	- A zone is the administrative authority over a portion of that hierarchy
	- You can own a domain name, but that doesn't mean you server the DNS reords for it yourself - that's the zone's job.
	- Example (The global DNS tree)
	.
	- com
		- google
			- www
			- mail
		- example
			- www

	- Now look at zones (who manages what)
		- Zone 1: The root zone "."
		- Zone 2: The "Com" zone (managed by verisign)
		- Zone 3: The "google.com" zone (managed by Google DNS servers)
		- Zone 4: The "example.com" zone (managed by Example corp)
	- Even though www.google.com is a domain name, the zone that contains it is google.com
	Things		Is it a name?	Is it a zone?	Why
	google.com	Domain		Zone		It has its own SOA + NS
	www.google.com	Domain		Not a zone	No separate authority

- How We Know SOmething is a Zone
	- A zone has:
		1. An SOA record ("Start of Authority)
		2. NS records listing its authoritative nameservers
	- If there is an SOA, it's a zone
	- Example zone top:
		google.com.	IN SOA	ns1.google.com.	dns-admin.google.com.	(...)
		google.com.	IN NS	ns1.google.com
		google.com.	IN NS	ns2.google.com

- Why Zones Exits
	- Zones allow the DNS hierarchy to be split and delegated
	- Without zones:
		- The root would need to store every hostname in the world (impossible)
	- With zones:
		- The Root only needs to know who controls .com
		- .com only needs to know who controls google.com
		- google.com controls everything below it
	- Zones decentralize control

- What are Zone Transfers?
	- Zones are usually stored on multiple authoritative nameservers
	- One is the primary (master), others are secondary (slaves)
	- A zone transfer is how the secondary servers copy the zone from the primary
	- Two Types:
		- AXFR	Full zone transfer	Copies entire zone database
		- IXFR	Incremental update	Only copy what changed since last time
	- Zone transfers use TCP not UDP

- Why Do We Need Zone Transfers?
	- Redundancy (If one server dies, others serve the zone)
	- Load sharing
	- Global reliability across networks
	(Zones are not stored on recursive resolvers, they are only on authoritative servers)

- What is a Zone used for?
	- A zone:
		- Stores all DNS records for the domain it manages
		- Answers queries authoritatively
		- Controls TTL policy
		- Signs data if using DNSSEC
		- Replicates to other authoritative servers via zone transfers
	- Essentially a database for that part of DNS

- Zones compared to everything around them
	Domain		Just a name in the hierarchy
	Zone		The admin authority over part of the hierarchy
	SOA		Declares where a zone begins and who manages it
	NS		Points to which servers provide the zone
	Zone File	The actual data (records) stored for that zone
	Zone Transfer	How that data gets copied to other authoritative servers










